/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*------------------------------ File set.dox ------------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_set */
/** \defgroup theory_set Theory of sets
 \ingroup area_set

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/set` realize the SET of chapter 8 of
 the book (on a boolean vector, on an unsorted and on a sorted list, on any
 dictionary) with its operations of two sets, and, beyond the book, three
 families that the engineering of the sets has added to it: the compressed
 bitmaps of Roaring, the intersections of sorted sequences of chapter 6,
 Set Intersection, of the book of Ferragina, and the filters, i.e., the sets
 that may answer yes for an item they do not hold. This page collects what the
 headers take for granted: the definitions and the notation (see
 \ref theory_set_notation), the realizations and the costs of their operations
 with the reason of each (see \ref theory_set_repr), the lower bounds and the
 algorithms of the operations of two sets (see \ref theory_set_ops and
 \ref theory_set_inter), and the analysis of the filters, from the lower bound
 on their space to the false positives of each of them (see
 \ref theory_set_filters), and of the filters of ranges (see
 \ref theory_set_range). The pseudocode of each algorithm is instead on the
 page of its header, in the comment of its function, together with the section
 of the book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_set_notation Definitions and notation

 A set \f$ A \f$ is a finite collection of distinct items of a universe
 \f$ U \f$; its operations are those of the concept `Set` of `ADT.h`, i.e.,
 `contains()`, `insert()` and `remove()`, with `forEach()`, which visits the
 items, and the four operations of two sets, the union \f$ A \cup B \f$, the
 intersection \f$ A \cap B \f$, the difference \f$ A \setminus B \f$ and the
 symmetric difference \f$ A \triangle B = ( A \setminus B ) \cup
 ( B \setminus A ) \f$, called `setUnion()`, `setIntersection()`,
 `setDifference()` and `setSymmetricDifference()`, since `union` is a keyword
 of C++ (chapter 8 of the book). A <em>multiset</em> may hold an item more
 than once, and its <em>multiplicity</em> is the number of its occurrences.

 A <em>filter</em> of a set \f$ S \f$ of \f$ n \f$ keys answers a query
 \f$ \mathrm{maybe}( x ) \f$ with no <em>false negative</em>, i.e.,
 \f$ \mathrm{maybe}( x ) \f$ is true for every \f$ x \in S \f$, and with a
 <em>false positive</em>, i.e., true for an \f$ x \notin S \f$, with a
 probability \f$ \varepsilon \f$ over the choice of its hash functions, the
 <em>false positive rate</em>. A filter is <em>static</em> when it is built
 once from \f$ S \f$, <em>incremental</em> when it takes insertions one at a
 time, and <em>dynamic</em> when it takes removals too.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$, \f$ m \f$</td>
     <td>the sizes of the two sets \f$ A \f$ and \f$ B \f$ of an operation,
         \f$ m \le n \f$ for the intersections; in a filter, \f$ n \f$ is the
         number of keys and \f$ m \f$ the bits, the places or the rows of its
         array, and in a hash table \f$ m \f$ is the size of the
         table</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ N \f$</td>
     <td>the dimension of a boolean vector, whose items are the integers of
         \f$ [ 0 , N ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \varepsilon \f$</td>
     <td>the false positive rate of a filter</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ k \f$, \f$ r \f$</td>
     <td>the number of hash functions of a filter of Bloom (\f$ k \f$ in
         <tt>BloomFilter</tt>, \f$ r \f$ in <tt>BloomVariants.h</tt>); in a
         Ribbon filter, \f$ r \f$ is the bits of a result</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ f \f$, \f$ b \f$</td>
     <td>the bits of a fingerprint of a cuckoo filter and of a binary fuse
         filter</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \alpha \f$</td>
     <td>the load of a table, the fraction of its places that are
         taken</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \Delta_j \f$</td>
     <td>the gap between the places of two consecutive items of the shorter
         list in the longer one, in a search by doubling</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ L \f$</td>
     <td>the length of a block of the two-level storage, of a bucket, or of
         a segment of a binary fuse filter</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_set_repr Realizations and their costs

 The library has six realizations of the SET, which differ in the universe
 they accept, in the space and in the time of the operations; the bounds of
 the hash tables are expected, over the choice of the hash function, and
 those of the operations of two sets are written for \f$ | A | = n \f$ and
 \f$ | B | = m \f$.

 <table>
 <caption>The realizations of the SET and the cost of their operations
          (chapter 8 of the book)</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Space</th>
     <th><tt>contains()</tt>, <tt>insert()</tt>, <tt>remove()</tt></th>
     <th><tt>min()</tt>, <tt>max()</tt></th><th><tt>forEach()</tt></th>
     <th>operations of two sets</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BoolSet</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( N ) \f$ bits</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( N ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( N ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( N ) \f$, \f$ \lceil N / 64 \rceil \f$ operations on
         words</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ListSet</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n ) \f$ (\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ for <tt>insertNew()</tt>)</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n m ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SortedListSet</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n + m ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ChainedHashSet</tt>, <tt>OpenHashSet</tt>,
         <tt>LinkedHashSet</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n + m ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n + m ) \f$, \f$ O( m ) \f$, \f$ O( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \max( m_A , n_A ) + \max( m_B , n_B ) ) \f$ expected</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>TreeSet</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n ) \f$, in order</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n + m \log( n + m ) ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>RoaringBitmap</tt></td>
     <td>at most \f$ 2 n \f$ bytes and \f$ | U | / 8 \f$ bytes, about</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log c + 4096 ) \f$ for \f$ c \f$ containers</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ to reach</td><td>\f$ O( n + c ) \f$</td>
     <td>a merge of the containers, each pair in
         \f$ O( 4096 ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 The boolean vector is the only realization whose operations do not depend
 on the items held but on the universe: it is preferable when \f$ N \f$ is
 not much larger than the sets, and its scans go 64 items at a time, a word
 per step, which leaves the bounds as they are and divides the time by up
 to 64. The unsorted list accepts any type with an equality, and its operations
 of two sets cost \f$ O( n m ) \f$, which is optimal under that only
 assumption (see \ref theory_set_ops); `insertNew()` costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
 since it skips the search of a duplicate, which the operations of two sets
 can do whenever the item is known to be new. The sorted list needs a total
 order, keeps its minimum and maximum at its ends, and merges two sets in
 \f$ O( n + m ) \f$ time. The hash tables give \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected time
 to the three operations on single items, and pay the size \f$ m \f$ of the
 table in every scan (all the \f$ m \f$ places are read, plus the
 \f$ n \f$ cells of the chains with chaining); `LinkedHashSet` threads a list
 through the items, so that its scans cost \f$ O( n ) \f$ in the order of
 insertion (exercise 8.9). The Red-Black tree keeps the order, and its
 operations of two sets insert the items of one set into a copy of the other,
 \f$ m \f$ insertions of \f$ O( \log( n + m ) ) \f$ (exercise 8.7).

 The Roaring bitmap cuts the universe of the integers of 32 bits into chunks
 of \f$ 2^{16} \f$ by their top 16 bits, and keeps each chunk that holds an
 item as a sorted array of the low 16 bits of its items when it holds at most
 4096 of them, and as a vector of \f$ 2^{16} \f$ bits otherwise. The two
 forms cost the same, 8 KB, exactly at 4096 items (\f$ 4096 \cdot 16 = 2^{16}
 \f$ bits), and the threshold takes the smaller one in either case; hence no
 container takes more than 8 KB, an item costs at most 2 bytes in an array,
 and a set takes at most about \f$ \min( 2 n , | U | / 8 ) \f$ bytes, the
 space of a sorted array on the sparse chunks and that of a boolean vector on
 the dense ones.

 \section theory_set_ops Operations of two sets

 Each operation of two sets is a scan of one set with a query of the other:
 the union inserts the items of \f$ B \f$ into a copy of \f$ A \f$, the
 intersection removes from a copy of the smaller set its items not in the
 other, the difference removes from a copy of \f$ A \f$ its items in
 \f$ B \f$, and the symmetric difference adds to \f$ A \setminus B \f$ the
 items of \f$ B \f$ not in \f$ A \f$ (`SetOperations.h`, on any set with a
 copy and a `forEach()`). Its cost is that of the copy, of the scan and of
 \f$ n \f$ or \f$ m \f$ queries, i.e., on a hash table of \f$ m_X \f$ places
 and \f$ n_X \f$ items, \f$ O( \max( m_A , n_A ) + \max( m_B , n_B ) ) \f$
 expected (exercise 8.6).

 <em>Lemma 1 (the sets with an equality only).</em> If the items can only be
 compared for equality, every algorithm that computes \f$ A \cap B \f$ makes
 \f$ n m \f$ comparisons in the worst case.

 Indeed, let an adversary answer "different" to every comparison. If the
 algorithm stops without comparing some \f$ a \in A \f$ with some
 \f$ b \in B \f$, the input in which \f$ a = b \f$, all the other items being
 distinct, agrees with every answer given, so that the algorithm returns the
 same result on it, while the intersection of the two inputs differs by
 \f$ a \f$. The same argument holds for the union, the difference and the
 symmetric difference; hence the \f$ O( n m ) \f$ of `ListSet` is optimal
 (exercises 8.1 and 8.2).

 With a total order and the sets sorted, the four operations are merges:
 the two lists are scanned together, the smaller of their two current items
 is examined and passed, and an item of \f$ A \f$ only, of \f$ B \f$ only, or
 of both goes to the result if the operation takes it (`merge()` of
 `SortedListSet.h`, section 8.3). Each comparison passes at least one item,
 hence at most \f$ n + m \f$ comparisons; on lists, which give no access but
 by a scan, this is optimal, and the result is sorted as it is written, each
 item appended at the end in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$. On sorted arrays the
 intersection can do better, as \ref theory_set_inter shows.

 \section theory_set_inter Intersections of sorted sequences

 The intersection of two sorted sequences is the core of the answer to a
 query of several words by an inverted index, each word having the sorted
 list of the documents that contain it (Ferragina, chapter 6, Set
 Intersection); there \f$ m \le n \f$ and the two lists may differ in length by
 orders of magnitude, and `SortedIntersection.h` gives the algorithms of
 that chapter.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the lower bound of the intersection).</em> An algorithm that
 intersects a sorted array of \f$ n \f$ items with one of \f$ m \le n \f$
 items by comparisons makes, in the worst case,
 \f[
   \log_2 \binom{n}{m} \ge m \log_2 \frac{n}{m} \tag{1}
 \f]
 comparisons.

 Indeed, when \f$ B \subseteq A \f$ the algorithm must tell which of the
 \f$ \binom{n}{m} \f$ subsets of \f$ A \f$ of \f$ m \f$ items it is, and a
 tree of binary comparisons with that many leaves has depth at least the
 logarithm of their number; the inequality is that of
 \f$ \binom{n}{m} \ge ( n / m )^m \f$. The merge is optimal when \f$ m \f$ and
 \f$ n \f$ are close, since then (1) is of the order of \f$ n + m \f$, and is
 far from it when \f$ m \ll n \f$.

 A binary search in \f$ A \f$ of each item of \f$ B \f$ costs
 \f$ O( m \log n ) \f$, which is better than the merge when
 \f$ m = o( n / \log n ) \f$. The mutual partition of Baeza-Yates searches
 the median of the shorter list in the longer one, which splits both lists
 into two pairs of shorter lists, and recurs on each pair with the roles
 swapped when the part of the longer list is the shorter; its cost is
 \f$ O( m ( 1 + \log( n / m ) ) ) \f$, which meets (1). The search by doubling
 reaches the same bound with no recursion: each item of \f$ B \f$ is searched
 in \f$ A \f$ from the place of the previous one, by probes at the distances
 \f$ 1 , 2 , 4 , \ldots \f$ and then a binary search in the last gap (Bentley
 and Yao), which costs \f$ O( 1 + \log \Delta_j ) \f$ for an item found
 \f$ \Delta_j \f$ places after the previous one. Since the gaps sum to at
 most \f$ n \f$ and the logarithm is concave,
 \f[
   \sum_{j=1}^m ( 1 + \log_2 \Delta_j ) \le m + m \log_2 \frac{n}{m} ,
   \tag{2}
 \f]
 the sum of the logarithms of \f$ m \f$ numbers of given sum being largest
 when they are equal. The two-level storage keeps in an array \f$ A' \f$ the
 first item of each block of \f$ L \f$ items of \f$ A \f$; a merge of
 \f$ A' \f$ with \f$ B \f$ tells the block where each item of \f$ B \f$ may
 be, and each block met is merged with its items of \f$ B \f$, in
 \f$ O( n / L + m L ) \f$ time, which is \f$ O( \sqrt{n m} ) \f$ for
 \f$ L = \sqrt{n / m} \f$; on the disk, with blocks of \f$ B \f$ items, a
 block of \f$ A \f$ met costs at most \f$ \lceil L / B \rceil + 1 \f$ reads.
 Finally, the permuted buckets of Sanders and Transier map the universe
 \f$ [ 0 , 2^w ) \f$ by the permutation \f$ \pi( x ) = ( a x + c ) \bmod 2^w
 \f$, \f$ a \f$ odd so that \f$ \pi \f$ is invertible, and split each list by
 the top \f$ \ell = \lceil \log_2( n / L ) \rceil \f$ bits of the images into
 buckets of about \f$ L \f$ items; an image of the shorter list is looked
 for in the bucket of the longer one named by its own top bits, so that a
 bucket of \f$ A \f$ is read only if \f$ B \f$ has an item in it, at most
 once, and the time is \f$ O( m + \min\{ n , m L \} ) \f$ expected.

 <table>
 <caption>The intersections of a sorted list of \f$ n \f$ items and one of
          \f$ m \le n \f$ items (Ferragina, chapter 6)</caption>
 <tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Function</th><th>Time</th>
     <th>Preferable</th></tr>
 <tr><td>merge</td><td><tt>intersectMerge()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( n + m ) \f$</td><td>\f$ m \f$ close to \f$ n \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>binary searches</td><td><tt>intersectBinarySearch()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( m \log n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ m = o( n / \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>mutual partition</td><td><tt>intersectMutualPartition()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( m ( 1 + \log( n / m ) ) ) \f$</td>
     <td>optimal by (1), with the cost of a recursion</td></tr>
 <tr><td>doubling</td><td><tt>intersectDoubling()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( m ( 1 + \log( n / m ) ) ) \f$ by (2)</td>
     <td>optimal, with no recursion</td></tr>
 <tr><td>two levels</td><td><tt>intersectTwoLevel()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( n / L + m L ) \f$</td><td>on the disk, few reads</td></tr>
 <tr><td>permuted buckets</td><td><tt>PermutedBuckets</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( m + \min\{ n , m L \} ) \f$ expected</td>
     <td>lists of similar length, not sorted beforehand</td></tr>
 </table>

 The bounds count comparisons, and on a modern processor the branches that
 the comparisons decide cost more than the comparisons, since those of a
 merge of two lists that interleave at random are mispredicted half of the
 time. `VectorIntersection.h` therefore merges with no branch, the indices
 advanced by the results of the comparisons, or compares a block of 8 items
 of each list all against all with the vector instructions; on lists of very
 different lengths it searches by doubling, as above, and on the integers of
 32 bits through a window of 64 items, whose moves the processor predicts.
 The bounds stay those of the table, and the time falls by a constant factor
 (Schlegel, Willhalm and Lehner; Lemire, Boytsov and Kurz).

 \section theory_set_filters Filters

 A filter trades exactness for space: it does not store the keys, and can
 therefore take fewer bits than any exact set, but no fewer than the
 following bound allows.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the space of a filter).</em> A filter of any \f$ n \f$ keys of
 a universe \f$ U \f$, with \f$ n \ll | U | \f$ and false positive rate
 \f$ \varepsilon \f$, takes at least
 \f[
   n \log_2 \frac{1}{\varepsilon} \tag{3}
 \f]
 bits (Carter, Floyd, Gill, Markowsky and Wegman).

 Indeed, a filter of \f$ s \f$ bits has at most \f$ 2^s \f$ states, and a
 state answers yes on a set of about \f$ n + \varepsilon | U | \f$ keys,
 which contains at most \f$ \binom{n + \varepsilon | U |}{n} \f$ sets of
 \f$ n \f$ keys; since each of the \f$ \binom{| U |}{n} \f$ sets of \f$ n \f$
 keys must be contained in the answers of some state,
 \f[
   2^s \ge \binom{| U |}{n} \Big/ \binom{n + \varepsilon | U |}{n} \approx
   \Bigl( \frac{| U |}{\varepsilon | U |} \Bigr)^n = \varepsilon^{-n}
 \f]
 for \f$ n \ll \varepsilon | U | \f$, which is (3). The filters below differ
 by how close they come to (3), by the operations they take, and by the lines
 of cache that a query reads.

 <em>Lemma 4 (the filter of Bloom).</em> Let a filter of \f$ m \f$ bits set,
 for each of \f$ n \f$ keys, the bits of \f$ k \f$ hash functions taken as
 independent and uniform. A bit is still 0 with probability
 \f$ p = ( 1 - 1 / m )^{k n} \approx e^{-k n / m} \f$, and the false positive
 rate is
 \f[
   \varepsilon = ( 1 - p )^k \approx \bigl( 1 - e^{-k n / m} \bigr)^k ,
   \tag{4}
 \f]
 least for \f$ k = ( m / n ) \ln 2 \f$, where
 \f$ \varepsilon = 2^{-k} \approx 0.6185^{m / n} \f$; the filter then takes
 \f$ m = n \log_2( 1 / \varepsilon ) / \ln 2 \approx 1.44 \, n \log_2( 1 /
 \varepsilon ) \f$ bits, i.e., 1.44 times (3).

 Indeed, a key out of \f$ S \f$ is a false positive when its \f$ k \f$ bits
 are all set, each with probability \f$ 1 - p \f$, which gives (4) up to the
 dependence of the bits, negligible for large \f$ m \f$. With
 \f$ k = - ( m / n ) \ln p \f$ one has
 \f$ \ln \varepsilon = - ( m / n ) \ln p \, \ln( 1 - p ) \f$, which is
 symmetric in \f$ p \f$ and \f$ 1 - p \f$ and hence least at
 \f$ p = 1 / 2 \f$, i.e., when half of the bits are set; then
 \f$ \varepsilon = 2^{-k} \f$ with \f$ k = ( m / n ) \ln 2 \f$, and solving
 for \f$ m \f$ gives the space. For \f$ \varepsilon = 0.01 \f$ the
 filter takes about 9.6 bits per key. The \f$ k \f$ places of `BloomFilter`
 are \f$ h_1 + i h_2 \bmod m \f$, \f$ i = 0 , \ldots , k - 1 \f$, of two
 hashes only, which Kirsch and Mitzenmacher show to leave (4) unchanged
 asymptotically.

 The variants of `BloomVariants.h` and `BlockedBloomFilter.h` change one
 assumption at a time. The partitioned filter gives each hash a part of
 \f$ m / k \f$ bits of its own, so that the \f$ k \f$ bits of a key are
 distinct: a bit of a part is 0 with probability \f$ ( 1 - k / m )^n \approx
 e^{-k n / m} \f$, and (4) holds with a slightly smaller variance (Grandi).
 The spectral filter of Cohen and Matias keeps \f$ m \f$ counters instead of
 bits, adds the multiplicity of a key to its counters and estimates it by
 the least of them: each counter of \f$ x \f$ has received all the
 occurrences of \f$ x \f$, so that the estimate is never below the
 multiplicity, and it is above it only if every counter of \f$ x \f$ is
 shared with another key, i.e., with the probability (4); a counter that
 reaches its largest value sticks there, so that a removal never makes a
 false negative (the counting filter of Fan, Cao, Almeida and Broder). The
 compressed filter of Mitzenmacher is sent through a channel compressed by
 an arithmetic coder, in \f$ m \, H( p ) \f$ bits, \f$ H \f$ the binary
 entropy; for a given number of bits sent, a larger filter with fewer
 hashes, whose bits are sparser, gives fewer false positives than the filter
 of the optimal \f$ k \f$. Two machines intersect \f$ A \f$ and \f$ B \f$ by
 sending the filter of \f$ A \f$ and receiving back the keys \f$ Q \f$ of
 \f$ B \f$ that it accepts, \f$ A \cap B \subseteq Q \f$, about
 \f$ | A \cap B | + \varepsilon | B | \f$ keys instead of the whole of
 \f$ A \f$ (`bloomIntersection()`).

 The blocked filter of Putze, Sanders and Singler puts the bits of a key in
 one block of 256 bits, one line of cache, instead of \f$ k \f$ lines
 anywhere. The blocks then get a Poisson number of keys of mean
 \f$ \lambda = 256 n / m \f$, and a block of \f$ j \f$ keys, which sets one
 bit of each of its 8 words of 32 bits per key, answers yes with
 probability \f$ ( 1 - ( 1 - 1 / 32 )^j )^8 \f$, so that
 \f[
   \varepsilon = \sum_{j \ge 0} e^{-\lambda} \frac{\lambda^j}{j!}
   \Bigl( 1 - \Bigl( 1 - \frac{1}{32} \Bigr)^j \Bigr)^8 ; \tag{5}
 \f]
 the crowded blocks answer yes more often than the sparse ones save, and
 (5) is above (4) with the same bits, e.g., 1.26 per cent against 0.82 at 10
 bits per key, the price of one miss of the cache per query.

 The filters of fingerprints keep, for each key, a fingerprint of \f$ f \f$
 bits of its hash, and a key out of \f$ S \f$ is a false positive when its
 fingerprint equals one of those it is compared with. In the cuckoo filter of
 Fan, Andersen, Kaminsky and Mitzenmacher a key has two buckets of 4
 places, the second being the first XOR a hash of the fingerprint, so that
 a fingerprint can be moved to its other bucket with no key at hand; a
 query compares at most 8 fingerprints, each equal to that of the key with
 probability \f$ 1 / ( 2^f - 1 ) \f$, and at the load \f$ \alpha \f$
 \f[
   \varepsilon = 1 - \Bigl( 1 - \frac{1}{2^f - 1} \Bigr)^{8 \alpha} \le
   \frac{8 \alpha}{2^f - 1} , \tag{6}
 \f]
 in \f$ f / \alpha \f$ bits per key; insertions by evictions reach a load of
 about 0.95, and the filter takes removals. The prefix filter of Even, Even
 and Morrison keeps the smallest 25 fingerprints of each bin of 32 bytes, in
 a pocket dictionary of Bercea and Even, and sends the others to a small
 blocked filter, the spare, which a query reads only when its fingerprint is
 above the largest one of a full bin; with \f$ m \f$ bins at the load
 \f$ \alpha \f$, the load of a bin is binomial of mean \f$ 25 \alpha \f$, the
 spare gets at most about \f$ n / \sqrt{2 \pi \cdot 25} \f$, i.e., 8 per
 cent, of the keys at \f$ \alpha = 1 \f$ (by the bound of Stirling on the
 binomial at its mean), and a query reads one line of cache in almost every
 case.

 The static filters solve a linear system. The binary fuse filter of Graf and
 Lemire gives each key 3 places of an array \f$ F \f$ of fingerprints of
 \f$ b \f$ bits, in 3 consecutive segments, and asks that
 \f$ F[ h_0 ] \oplus F[ h_1 ] \oplus F[ h_2 ] = f( h ) \f$; the system is
 solved by peeling, i.e., by repeatedly taking out a place reached by one key
 only, which is the last to be assigned for that key, and the peeling
 succeeds with high probability when the array has about \f$ 1.125 n \f$
 places for large \f$ n \f$ (\f$ 1.23 n \f$ for 3 places anywhere, as in the
 xor filters), the spatial coupling of the segments lowering the threshold
 (Dietzfelbinger and Walzer). The Ribbon filter of Dillinger and Walzer gives
 each key one equation over \f$ \mathbb{F}_2 \f$ with 64 coefficients from a
 random start, solved by the Gauss elimination of a banded matrix in the
 order of the starts, in \f$ r ( 1 + \varepsilon' ) \f$ bits per key for a
 small overhead \f$ \varepsilon' \f$. In both, a key out of \f$ S \f$ meets
 its equation when a fixed value equals its fingerprint, which is uniform and
 independent of it; hence \f$ \varepsilon = 2^{-b} \f$, or \f$ 2^{-r} \f$,
 and the space is within a factor 1.125, or \f$ 1 + \varepsilon' \f$, of (3).

 <table>
 <caption>The filters of the library, at about 8 bits of fingerprint, and
          their space with respect to (3)</caption>
 <tr><th>Filter</th><th>Operations</th><th>Bits per key</th>
     <th>Lines of cache per query</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BloomFilter</tt></td><td>insertions</td>
     <td>\f$ 1.44 \log_2( 1 / \varepsilon ) \f$</td><td>\f$ k \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BlockedBloomFilter</tt></td><td>insertions, union</td>
     <td>above that of <tt>BloomFilter</tt> by (5)</td><td>1</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SpectralBloomFilter</tt></td>
     <td>insertions, removals, multiplicities</td>
     <td>that of <tt>BloomFilter</tt>, a counter per bit</td>
     <td>\f$ r \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CuckooFilter</tt></td><td>insertions, removals</td>
     <td>\f$ f / \alpha \f$, \f$ \varepsilon \f$ by (6)</td><td>2</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>PrefixFilter</tt></td><td>insertions, up to \f$ n \f$ keys</td>
     <td>about 11.55 for \f$ \varepsilon \approx 0.39 \f$ per cent</td>
     <td>1, rarely 2</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BinaryFuseFilter</tt></td><td>static</td>
     <td>\f$ 1.125 b \f$, \f$ \varepsilon = 2^{-b} \f$</td><td>3</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>RibbonFilter</tt></td><td>static</td>
     <td>\f$ r ( 1 + \varepsilon' ) \f$, \f$ \varepsilon = 2^{-r} \f$</td>
     <td>2 for a key out of the set</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_set_range Range filters

 A <em>range filter</em> of a set \f$ S \f$ of \f$ n \f$ keys of the
 universe \f$ [ 0 , 2^{64} ) \f$ tells whether a range \f$ [ a , b ] \f$ may
 hold a key of \f$ S \f$: it answers true for every range that holds one,
 and for an empty range of length \f$ \ell = b - a + 1 \f$ with probability
 \f$ \varepsilon( \ell ) \f$. A filter of points answers a range by asking
 its \f$ \ell \f$ points, i.e., with \f$ \ell \f$ queries and up to
 \f$ \ell \f$ times their false positives, while the filters of ranges built
 on tries (SuRF), on filters of prefixes (Rosetta) or on learned models
 (SNARF, Proteus) answer in fewer steps but fail on the correlated queries,
 the empty ranges close to a key, where their false positives go to 1.
 `GrafiteFilter` of `GrafiteFilter.h` has a bound that holds for every set
 and every query, the only randomness being that of a hash.

 With \f$ B \f$ bits per key, Grafite reduces the universe to
 \f$ [ 0 , r ) \f$, \f$ r = n \, 2^{B-2} \f$, by the hash
 \f[
   h( x ) = \bigl( q( \lfloor x / r \rfloor ) + x \bigr) \bmod r , \tag{7}
 \f]
 \f$ q \f$ a pairwise independent hash of the blocks of \f$ r \f$
 consecutive keys into \f$ [ 0 , r ) \f$, and keeps the codes \f$ h( S ) \f$
 by Elias-Fano, in \f$ n B \f$ bits. Inside a block, (7) is a rotation, so
 that a range of \f$ \ell \le r \f$ keys, which meets at most two blocks, has
 for image at most two cyclic ranges of \f$ [ 0 , r ) \f$ of \f$ \ell \f$
 codes in all, which a successor query of Elias-Fano answers in
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ each.

 <em>Lemma 5 (the range filter of Grafite, Costa, Ferragina and
 Vinciguerra).</em> For every set \f$ S \f$ and every range of
 \f$ \ell \f$ keys,
 \f[
   \varepsilon( \ell ) \le \min\Bigl( 1 , \frac{ n \ell }{ r } \Bigr) =
   \min\Bigl( 1 , \frac{ \ell }{ 2^{B-2} } \Bigr) . \tag{8}
 \f]

 Indeed, a key of \f$ S \f$ in the range has its code in the image of the
 range, and there is no false negative. A key \f$ x \f$ of \f$ S \f$ out of
 the range, in a block that the range meets, has its code out of the image
 of the part of the range in its block, since (7) is one to one on a block;
 with respect to the part of the range in the other block, and to all of it
 when \f$ x \f$ is in a block that the range does not meet, the code of
 \f$ x \f$ is uniform on \f$ [ 0 , r ) \f$ given \f$ q \f$ of the other
 block, \f$ q \f$ being pairwise independent, and falls in a stretch of at
 most \f$ \ell \f$ codes with probability at most \f$ \ell / r \f$. The union
 bound over the \f$ n \f$ keys gives (8), which generalizes the construction
 of Goswami, Grønlund, Larsen and Pagh to the ranges of any length; the
 lower bound of the same paper, \f$ n \log_2( \ell / \varepsilon ) - O( n )
 \f$ bits, is within 2 bits per key of Grafite.

 `BucketingFilter`, the heuristic of the same paper, keeps instead the
 buckets of \f$ s = \lceil 2^{64} / ( n \, 2^{B-2} ) \rceil \f$ keys that
 hold a key of \f$ S \f$, and answers true when a kept bucket meets the
 range. It needs no hash and is faster, and it is exact on queries drawn at
 random independently of the keys; but an empty range inside the bucket of
 a key, at distance below \f$ s \f$ from it, is always a false positive, so
 that no bound like (8) holds for every query.

